From 5f2f40ed2838f0cb3597a06bae5f516cde350eac Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Campos Ramos <danielcamposramos.68@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 22:30:17 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] comp/main: peek window shows both eyes undistorted, side by
 side, and declares it

XRT_WINDOW_PEEK=both blitted the distortion target, with a TODO asking for
the undistorted image. Blit the two undistorted eye scratch images instead,
left eye in the left half of the window and right eye in the right half:
full side by side, left eye first, the layout HDMI 1.4 calls 3D structure 3
and the one stereo desktops take. The old picture, as the headset shows it,
stays available as XRT_WINDOW_PEEK=display.

The window is created at one eye's size. With libstereo-declare available
(XRT_HAVE_STEREO_DECLARE, pkg-config stereo-declare) the window tells the
desktop what it holds, so a stereo compositor (KWin on Sparky Stereo OS)
shows each half to its eye on a 3D television, projector or monitor while
the headset runs: a stereo spectator. On X11 the window manager then gives
the window two eyes' worth of pixels, so the blit follows the swapchain's
extent and the swapchain is recreated only when the window itself changes
size. On Wayland, where SDL offers no viewport, the window is widened to
hold both eyes side by side.

Verified headless (KWin virtual output, Monado's simulated headset,
lavapipe, an OpenXR client clearing the left eye red and the right eye
blue): the capture's left eye shows the red view and its right eye the blue
view in a 640x720 window, no swapped pixels, nothing outside the window
differing between the eyes.

Assisted-by: Claude Fable via Claude Code
